{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/115647-using-a-different-system","id":115647,"title":"Using a different system","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-18T19:18:21-07:00","id":115647,"title":"Using a different system","content":"So normally I don't ask questions like this but how would altering Changeling to work more like Demon powers work? I've been thinking about it for a while and it just seems like a better mechanical system for changelings power-wise. It fits the grab bag nature of changeling contracts without stifling it to a set in stone power system. It honestly feels more like a contract with the wyld for powers than the current system does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-18T19:40:43-07:00","id":115659,"title":"","content":"Unless you feel like making an entire set of Embed-esque categories that still fit the theme of &quot;you have some degree of pull with this aspect of reality,&quot; I don't forsee this working out well. The Fae being their bargains (in a manner that incidentally causes them to greatly resemble powerful spirits and other godlike beings) presently evokes this through constitution-level Contract initiation being akin to free-improv within a motif-set that gets reduced to a more fixed chain of tricks Earthside. Turning that into a grab-bag does nothing of much use when &quot;grab-bag of unrelated powers&quot; is largely the point of Goblin Contracts as an umbrella classification.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-18T19:50:20-07:00","id":115664,"title":"","content":"The problem is that the changeling set is already intended to be a grab bag of powers it just also tried to be a coherent theme. In the end, it tried to do both and failed. The powers are already grab bag and overly narrow in theme.Outside of the catches, it also doesn't feel very Fate driven. Or a set of tools given by making a contract with the Wyld. Making an entire set of embed-esque categories would still be less work than revamping every single contract to GMC standards.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-18T20:49:38-07:00","id":115687,"title":"","content":"I was actually thinking that each contract could have two minor clauses and one major one. The major one being essentially five dots but with a clause requirement. While the two minor ones would be fairly powerful but more like the Demon embeds. Unlike Demons they could have multiple Ciphers but it would just be a way to get unique powers rather than a super powerful clause. Like Demon though they would just be Examples rather than the definite thing. I'd also make goblin contracts something else but I personally don't like them and I've never seen anyone purchase them in a game or with any plan to purchase one. I'd rather just tie major Clauses with a required Catched.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T20:58:33-07:00","id":116323,"title":"","content":"You've seriously never seen someone use a Goblin Contract? Huh. I'm hard pressed to think of a PC in my group that doesn't have one that they use at least semi-regularly.<br /> <br /> Personally, I've always felt that the existing Contracts actually captured the feeling of highly precise agreements with differing aspects of reality quite well. That &quot;overly narrow in theme&quot; aspect you mentioned suits the concept perfectly, in my opinion.<br /> <br /> I've not yet had a chance to look at Demon, so I'm not entirely sure what the exact change is that you're aiming for. However, the idea that the categories just be examples rather than something defined sounds like the exact opposite of what a magic system called &quot;Contracts&quot; should be like. In my experience, contracts (as in legal ones) are as precise as the parties involved can make them. Anything vague has to be stamped out as quickly as possible. Since the Contracts come off in the book as being signed with all fae-kind (or possibly the Wyrd), rather than with individuals, the idea that they might vary from person to person seems antithetical to the concept of the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T21:42:59-07:00","id":116334,"title":"","content":"People in my group rarely use powers except for my character. Benefits to Drawbacks for goblin contracts are all over the place. They might be cheap but the group is hard pressed to purchase them especially the ones that have a permanent willpower cost associated to it.<br /> <br /> <br /> Examples means that it's not the only ones that exist and you can make up more. The idea that they are personal is actually more close to the concept of the system because each changeling makes a a contract with the wyld on an individual basis rather than a group based one. The Durance which causes the changeling to become a changeling is highly personal and unique. It would make better sense for their powers to be more unique rather than less unique to themselves. The Fae make pacts with fundamental forces of nature and they do so in a way that is again unique to them. Each Fae is different from one another as are their powers if the books are to be believed. The problem with contracts is that they contradict each other in shape and tone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T22:30:31-07:00","id":116339,"title":"","content":"A quick note on the permanent willpower ones. Those are generally Contracts of Sacrifice and their ilk. I wouldn't expect the average player to have them. I wouldn't even expect the average NPC to have them. They're generally powers that fit a particularly niche character concept (the witch sacrificing her own health to slay a foe, for example). The Goblin Contracts my group uses are more along the lines of Invisible Guardian, Delay Harm, and that one that lets you spot lies at the cost of having to speak the truth (can't recall the name).<br /> <br /> And I don't believe the system in which Changelings gain powers is remotely personal. The durance is, absolutely. But the reason the Changelings gain powers from it is because their durance makes them part-fae. This is true for all Changelings, regardless of their experiences in the durance. And as a result of being part-fae, they are now &quot;signatories of the second party&quot; (the book uses words to that effect, from memory). That is to say, the Wyrd considers them Fae, and they are now privy to the Contracts and Clauses that were already signed and agreed upon by the True Fae in their dealings with reality.<br /> <br /> This means that Changelings had no part in the initial creation of the powers they use. They are merely getting access to an agreement signed by other parties by virtue of their new fae nature. The Contracts and Clauses existed prior to the Changeling being able to use them, and the Changeling has no say in what these powers will do. They're all working within the framework of an already existing pact.<br /> <br /> As for the True Fae, I think you're misreading something. The books mention that the True Fae are capable of almost free-form powers by renegotiating their powers with reality on the fly. That's something different from the way Contracts work for Changelings. The True Fae are the original signatories, after all. They have a right to go back and work out an extra deal on the side when it suits them.<br /> <br /> Also, the True Fae are not really &quot;different&quot; from one another. I mean, yes, the defining feature of each Fae is that they've managed to define themselves against the Wyrd, as a unique individual. But they are still a part of the Wyrd, formed solely from the Wyrd and composed of none of the extra human bits that Changelings are lugging around. The books imply therefore that, on some level, Contracts are forged between the Wyrd and reality. The Fae get access to them, and the right to reshape them, because they are the Wyrd (and they don't get access to all of them because part of defining yourself as apart from the Wyrd is not being as all-powerful as you can be). Changelings have a bit of the Wyrd about them, so they can sneakily get access to some of the agreed upon powers, but as they are not the Wyrd themselves they cannot reshape powers like the Fae can. They can only work within the existing structure.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T23:48:28-07:00","id":116364,"title":"","content":"I think we'll just have to agree to disagree. I see contracts as a reflection of a fundamental forces but clauses as flexible. The thing about the wyrd is that they can be all-powerful. Look at the dragon. He's pretty much undefeatable without massive cheesing and inside the hedge it's impossible without another Fae doing it for you. They can reshape powers to suit their needs at any time as they ult themselves for whatever reason.<br /> <br /> My other problem is that the system is Archaic and honestly doesn't work for changelings. It works for vampires but as we got more types and different formats for powers it just shows it's age. It suffers from having some rather lackluster powers like Dreamsteps and cobblethought and the unusable contracts known as Hearth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T00:47:51-07:00","id":116403,"title":"","content":"Wow, my group seems to be the exact opposite to yours in terms of Contracts. Well, yeah, Dreamsteps and Cobblethought don't see much use (and I do agree that the Contracts of Dreams are the most poorly designed Contracts in the game), but I have one player who adores Hearth. A second level Clause that gives you or a friend +4 dice any time you need it for 1 Glamour? A guaranteed success whenever you need it? Those two Clauses are amazing. The guaranteed Exceptional Success even sees some use, although since our games rarely jump a year at any time it gets treated as a once-per-game-per-player super move. It's still pretty fantastic to have when you need an NPC to accomplish something.<br /> <br /> I think we will need to agree to disagree. You state that this system doesn't work for Changelings, but I honestly think it is far and away more appropriate for Changelings than it is for any other splat. Vampires are the ones I would expect to have a grab bag of largely unrelated powers. Changelings have formal Contracts with reality, a list of Clauses they can enact to bend the world in particular, pre-determined ways. Lists of powers you buy in order is very, very appropriate.<br /> <br /> Regarding the dragon, he is explicitly stated to be a special case due to the iron embedded next to his heart. Fae in the real world are capped at Wyrd 6 usually. They need some kind of real threat to define them. If they ever go all-powerful, they cease to exist. The dragon can only survive doing so because he is perpetually on the very precipice of death. He doesn't need to be weak enough for a Changeling or mortal to kill, because the iron provides him with all the threat he needs. That's not &quot;whatever reason&quot;, that is &quot;if the Fae is challenged by some facet of existence that extra power will not help them overcome&quot;. There is no going &quot;ult&quot; for Fae when faced by something they can't immediately overcome. If they were to do that, they would simply fade back into the Wyrd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T01:29:26-07:00","id":116416,"title":"","content":"Hearth is awful because not only are the higher powers practically useless it's cardinal sin is that is boring as fuck. That it's THE Fate based power and it's boring as fuck is awful.<br /> <br /> The system doesn't work in my opinion because it's clear that changeling was intended to be a grab bag of powers but it does it an extremely clunky way. A number of powers don't feel like they belong or they are boring to use. I just think a new system would be better than trying to make the old system work.<br /> <br /> It depends on what book you read because Fae are godlike beings in some books Out of changeling perspective and than weak in other books. There's nothing coherent about it which would work if it was in game but it's not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T04:10:34-07:00","id":116437,"title":"","content":"Hearth is about powers based on luck. Luck is an ephemeral element already represented by the game's basic system. While it's not the best, the Ban system allows for some fun blessings/curses to muck with a player (or for a player to muck with an NPC). I agree that it's not the most interesting, but it is effective and I can't imagine anything more interesting given the subject without straying into Talecrafting levels of absurdity.<br /> <br /> Perhaps you should define a bit better by what you mean by &quot;grab bag of powers&quot;. That'll help people give you suggestions on how to get the effect you desire. Personally, I don't get any such impression from Changeling. Reading the corebook, I get the image that Changeling Contracts and Clauses are meant to be highly precise tricks and deals Changelings have with the world that let them perform magic that the world has allowed them to do. The sequential purchasing system emulates this well enough, especially with the higher levels costing more experience (a kind of &quot;you must give more of yourself for the greater power&quot; kind of deal). Personally, I never got the impression that Changeling powers would ever be as disorganised as a &quot;grab bag&quot;. We're talking Contracts and Clauses, a sort of magical piece of law signed between two parties. The result should feel like such. But from what I'm gathering, you would rather powers be the same price across the board (or split into two categories, greater and lesser powers, with different price points), and not grouped into set Contracts. Is that correct?<br /> <br /> As for the True Fae, they are always strong, and never truly godlike outside of Arcadia. And even in Arcadia, they agree to have particular weaknesses and to lose in the face of certain actions.The rules for creating them in Autumn Nightmares, the core book, and Equinox Road are all slightly different, but you can easily emulate both of the others by using the system found in Equinox Road. And there, a Fae Actor is rarely all that much more powerful than a high-end Changeling. Not precisely &quot;godlike&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T07:20:07-07:00","id":116489,"title":"","content":"Look at the fate Arcana or mummy. They have fate powers that are more than just straight up successes and are more interesting to play with. Hearth is just the same power five times slightly better than the previous.<br /> <br /> By that I mean that the changeling set is full of things like Elements and separation which are coherent and then it has things like Dreams and Thorn and Eternal Spring which feel like a bunch of random powers that don't really come together as a theme. Just a collection of random powers stapled together because sort of theme but not really. I've been referring to the clauses themselves not the contracts. I have no problem with Contracts I just have the issues with clauses and the structure of they are in.<br /> <br /> I remember a few true fae hitting 8 wyrd and having enough stats that it was practically impossible to fight them outside the dragon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T17:29:41-07:00","id":116756,"title":"","content":"Ah, well, I can definitely sympathise on the subject of some Clauses not being all that good. That's a bit different from the fundamental structure of the system, however. I'm also not so sure about Thorn and Eternal Spring lacking coherent themes. They seemed pretty much fine to me. Dreams is terrible though.<br /> <br /> Comparing Hearth to Mummy powers is an interesting one though. I'm not so sure that lowering target values is all that much more interesting than what Hearth does. It would probably make a decent replacement for the fourth dot of Hearth, but functionally it just means more successes. That just makes it a different way of carrying out the second dot of Hearth, with varying levels of power depending on the size of the dice pool. Not really any more or less &quot;interesting&quot;, just stronger.<br /> <br /> Personally, and this is probably due to my group having quite a lot of experience at this point (plus one high Wyrd character), the only single creature I've seen in any Changeling material that looks like it might give my players a serious fight is a hobgoblin, if we discount the dragon. Most Fae are no more powerful stat-wise than a Changeling of their Wyrd level.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T05:44:44-07:00","id":116901,"title":"","content":"Thorn is okay for the most part I think it's just one power that's off. Eternal Spring has two healing abilities, one weather one, one growing one, and one control all plants one. Not only does Control all plants belong in elements it doesn't mesh with the rest of the powers which have no combat applications. The fourth power has no practical application and the weather one while okay is like the last power should be in something else. And while I could break down everything into what I feel works and doesn't work it would be very long and tiring needless to say I find that it's not just in one or two powers it's in a large portion of them.<br /> <br /> Mummy has a lot of powers that aren't just dice bonuses they use fate to acquire merits, story hooks, and other things. It's not just stronger but it's done in a way that makes it feel like you are using fate for a purpose and just an out of game dice booster. Or in the 4 and 5 powers a way to make sure your enemies never get a coherent roll again.<br /> <br /> I'm not in a game where Fae get normal stats so it's colored my view on them but if I remember those stats are optional. More to the point, most of the game tends to ignore those stats. If it were possible to beat up the fae from ever taking a changeling, and with those stats it is, the entire gameline as a whole wouldn't make sense. Even then Equanox Roads(I believe) , which gives rules for fae put them in a position to wipe the floor with Props that can instantly kill people or force them to surrender, puts True Fae in and out of arcadia in much stronger position.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T16:47:43-07:00","id":117236,"title":"","content":"Not too many humans are going to put up a fight against a surprise attack by a being with magic powers and stats as high as 6. Not to mention that if the Fae come at someone unprepared (and since humans don't usually know of the Fae, they're all unprepared) then the odds are good that said human is just armed with their fists. Even a Strength 5, Brawl 5 character still does Bashing damage. Which is to say, they can't actually hurt the Fae. And while props are amazing, their existence doesn't (or rather, shouldn't) put a Fae's stats into the same realm as a dragon or make them impossible to defeat.<br /> <br /> If your GM is running Fae in a different manner from the books though, then your examples aren't really of any use except in your particular case. The fact is that outside of your game, the Fae are never undefeatable because to be so would be to render their existence meaningless. That, in turn, destroys the Fae just as well as killing it would, for all extents and purposes. The core book has an extremely powerful example of a Fae, but none of its stats are higher than 6. That's the default for most of the game line, so I'm not sure what you're referring to when you say that &quot;most of the game tends to ignore those stats&quot;. Equinox Road, as an end game supplement, has rules for making them stronger, but it is the book that explicitly states that a Fae that is too powerful just dissolves back into the Wyrd.<br /> <br /> All of which ties back into my original point, that Contracts are not individually struck between different fae creatures (True Fae, Changelings, Hobgoblins, whatever) and reality, but rather between the Wyrd as a whole and reality. This creates the set Contracts, which all Fae potentially have access to (but don't, for the &quot;can't be all-powerful reason discussed above&quot;), and Changelings can sneakily access by virtue of being part-fae.<br /> <br /> Anyway, like I said before, I can certainly sympathise with quibbles over individual powers. This topic is about the actual structure of the Changeling magic system though. While I can agree not all the Clauses are always perfect for their respective Contracts, and some aren't always that useful and/or interesting, I think the way Changeling magic works on a fundamental level is entirely appropriate to the background provided by the books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T17:38:28-07:00","id":117262,"title":"","content":"Yeah, but there contracts that help you figure out the future or could be made that invalidate fae grabbing people. Equinox roads is really what cements Fae as more than just Stats. And honestly, they shouldn't be beatable in combat. It should take a story to beat one.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think that powers could be made better. I think a different system could help with that rather than structuring it with the same system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T18:13:52-07:00","id":117281,"title":"","content":"Even a regular human can get away with kidnapping/murder with the right equipment and no discernible motivation. Imagine how easy it must be for creatures that are potentially stronger/faster/smarter than any human can possible be, can abscond to a different dimension, leave behind no evidence of their crime (up to and including a replacement for their victim), and have magical powers? Not to mention the fact that the Fae are potentially infinite, while the Changelings are just a small percentage of their victims (the majority, I imagine, never escape Arcadia). Those Clauses you spoke of that allow you to predict the future don't really help you determine if a Fae you have never encountered is about to kidnap a human you have never met. Changelings don't have the manpower to protect every mortal on Earth, or even any noteworthy percentage of mortals on Earth, from beings that can strike at any time, anywhere, to take any one. Let's face it, even a fae with no powers and stats that are perfectly normal for an average human can still just rock up with a needle full of tranquiliser, come out of the Hedge into someone's house and stick the needle in their neck before they wake up. Protecting a single human close to the player characters from being taken is a story unto itself, and it's entirely possible that the players could be successful. I disagree with the notion that the same process could be replicated to create any form of systematic defence against Fae activity.<br /> <br /> Now, in my game, the group has faced Fae precisely twice. Once was a player's Keeper, required a story of research to discover his weaknesses, and ended in a climatic confrontation where the Keeper was slain. This was all despite the fact that, the whole time, the Keeper was statted out in such a way that a group of three combat characters could quite easily have killed him. In fact, two of the group's three players were perfectly capable of tackling this Keeper in a one-on-one fight. He wasn't even that strong a fighter. If your GM needs his monsters to have stupidly high stats in order to force the players into a story to defeat them, then I'm afraid he's just not trying to play his monsters intelligently. Despite being fairly average stat-wise, this Keeper, who was not much better in a fight than any Wyrd 5 Changeling, still took out two of the party's three characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T19:56:22-07:00","id":117340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/115647-using-a-different-system?p=117281#post117281\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, in my game, the group has faced Fae precisely twice. Once was a player's Keeper, required a story of research to discover his weaknesses, and ended in a climatic confrontation where the Keeper was slain. This was all despite the fact that, the whole time, the Keeper was statted out in such a way that a group of three combat characters could quite easily have killed him. In fact, two of the group's three players were perfectly capable of tackling this Keeper in a one-on-one fight. He wasn't even that strong a fighter. If your GM needs his monsters to have stupidly high stats in order to force the players into a story to defeat them, then I'm afraid he's just not trying to play his monsters intelligently. Despite being fairly average stat-wise, this Keeper, who was not much better in a fight than any Wyrd 5 Changeling, still took out two of the party's three characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>You really don't know enough about my Game to say that. First off. Second off if keepers can't wipe the team then yes, it turns fighting a keeper from a tense moment into an anti-climatic one. I don't know if my Gm would agree on me with this but a Fae who can be beaten in combat by any group of changelings isn't really a good changeling antagonist. If all it takes is just a simple fight to beat one up then everything up to then and everything interesting about them is kind of lost, except for those one off moments, they should be taken out through clever ways not just through brute force. If the team can just beat one up. I know I'd pretty much lose interest and I'm pretty sure that's why Equinox Roads was created.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 04-21-2014, 11:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T23:09:12-07:00","id":117436,"title":"","content":"You're right, I have no idea what your game is like, but I do find it hard to believe that you need a monster to be capable of fighting the entire group for it to be anything but anti-climatic. You can give it Hobgoblin/Wisp retainers to slow down most of the group, for example. Or, better, you can play on the horror aspects of the game and run a monster that relies on separating the group and picking them off one by one. World of Darkness is a system that vastly preferences the side with the superior numbers, and a group is almost always going to have a fairly easy time rolling over any one monster in a straight fight, no matter how strong it is. To get a Fae that can't be beaten in a straight fight by a group of Changelings, especially if those Changelings are half-way competent player characters, you pretty much have to drop something like the dragon. The fact is that most Fae are not at this level. Even in Equinox Roads. A Fae with 10 in every conceivable stat, for example, still drops fairly fast to 3-4 characters with half-way decent combat stats using iron ranged weapons of some sort. Range to ignore defence, iron to ignore armour, assume a fairly low dice pool of 8 per combat character from 2-3 Attribute, 2-3 Skill, Specialty, and 2-3 Equipment bonus and add Willpower for somewhere between 2 and 4 successes per turn per character... if 4 characters get 4 successes and the Fae is &quot;only&quot; Size 6 Stamina 10 then they just killed it in one round.<br /> <br /> That doesn't mean the monster can't be terrifying, and that the final confrontation with a Fae can't be tense and exciting, even if they are comparatively &quot;weak&quot; by having no stat higher than 6. There was only one member of the group still standing by the time the confrontation came with the Keeper, and I can assure you that it was a tense fight. Raw stats, I would argue, are just about the last thing you need to make a story exciting and tense. I would wager most GMs with a good grasp of horror mechanics and a willingness to run their NPCs intelligently could create a gripping story with a primary antagonist in the form of a lone mortal with Strength 2, Weaponry 1, and an appropriate specialty. And I know this for a fact, because I've managed to do pretty much that using an Ensorcelled human. Even if the final fight with such a weak character can be exhilarating, if you pick the right location and circumstances and avoid having it be a front up gentleman's bout of boxing in a white room. If you can't do the same using a creature with all the heightened power of a Fae, I'm at a loss. Fae are strong as it is, and the only reason I can think of as to why you would need to make them stronger than they are in the books would be if you were trying to run the game like a D&amp;D dungeon crawl with the Fae as a boss monster at the end. That's a perfectly valid way of playing, but it means you're working in a framework a little bit different from the one the World of Darkness books seem to be assuming you'll be using.<br /> <br /> We're getting a way off-topic though. Suffice to say that citing the way Fae work in your game, which you admit is different from the way they work in the books, is not hugely relevant in a discussion about how to change the rules of the magic system in said book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T23:45:07-07:00","id":117449,"title":"","content":"My point is that the kind of game is a horror based game. Stating the unkillable kind of takes away from that (Except the Iron against a true fae). But it is off topic. I have put some work on it so I'll probably post ideas soon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-22T15:31:22-07:00","id":117694,"title":"","content":"Hmm...<br /> <br /> Here's an idea:<br /> <br /> Expand Pledgecrafting to let you make contracts with beings other than just Mortals and derivatives. Like, I dunno, be able to pick up some Influences or something by making a deal with fire or something.<br /> <br /> That way, if you want to have the Contract of Fire, you have to make a contract with fire.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-22T17:02:43-07:00","id":117736,"title":"","content":"...you mean Goblin Vows?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-23T10:48:14-07:00","id":117999,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>amechra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/115647-using-a-different-system?p=117694#post117694\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm...<br /> <br /> Here's an idea:<br /> <br /> Expand Pledgecrafting to let you make contracts with beings other than just Mortals and derivatives. Like, I dunno, be able to pick up some Influences or something by making a deal with fire or something.<br /> <br /> That way, if you want to have the Contract of Fire, you have to make a contract with fire.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't like that idea. Goblin Vows and the pledge system in general is honestly rather boring for what it is. It's ironically to mechanical for my taste. Forcing more mechanics to that system just bogs it down. The there's the cost of powers without using experience which can get bullshit really quickly with Changelings making week long pledges and switching powers at the drop of a hat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}